## 3. Corporate billing models

### Model A — One contract, one recurring billing obligation

Suitable when a corporate customer has one standardized service and one recurring amount.

```text
CORPORATE CUSTOMER
        |
        v
SERVICE CONTRACT
        |
        v
RECURRING BILLING
        |
        v
PAYMENT COLLECTION
        |
        v
ACCOUNT RECONCILIATION
```

### Model B — One corporate customer, multiple billing obligations

Suitable when a corporate customer receives multiple services or has different billing arrangements.

```text
           CORPORATE CUSTOMER
                   |
          +--------+--------+
          |        |        |
          v        v        v
       SERVICE A SERVICE B SERVICE C
          |        |        |
          v        v        v
       BILL A    BILL B    BILL C
          |        |        |
          v        v        v
       PAYMENT   PAYMENT   PAYMENT
          |        |        |
          +--------+--------+
                   |
                   v
          CORPORATE RECONCILIATION
```

Each obligation should retain its own billing reference even when the merchant reports them under one corporate account.

### Model C — Account-based periodic billing

Suitable when a customer is billed based on an agreed account-level cycle or calendar date.

Potential examples:

- Monthly service charges.
- Periodic facility fees.
- Corporate account charges.
- Agreed account billing cycles.

Evaluate FlexiBill Account Billing for this model.

### Model D — Contract-based billing with variable charges

Suitable when charges depend on contract terms, service quantities, or external calculations.

The merchant's contract and operational systems should calculate the commercial obligation. FlexiBill's role depends on the validated billing and integration model.

Do not assume native real-time usage metering or automatic enterprise contract interpretation.

